840 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 126 0 R
/T (Measured \(200,000 lines\): LBYL stays flat at about 0.016 s \(about 0.08 microseconds per line\) at any bad-line rate. EAFP costs 0.0247 s with 0.1% bad lines and rises to 0.0260 s \(1%\), 0.0319 s \(10%\) and 0.0586 s \(50%\). From 0.1% to 50% bad lines the extra 0.034 s over about 100,000 bad lines means roughly 0.3 microseconds per raised and caught exception. Part of EAFP's base cost is the extra function call to parse_line, not the try itself.)
/E (Measured \(200,000 lines\): LBYL stays flat at about 0.016 s \(about 0.08 microseconds per line\) at any bad-line rate. EAFP costs 0.0247 s with 0.1% bad lines and rises to 0.0260 s \(1%\), 0.0319 s \(10%\) and 0.0586 s \(50%\). From 0.1% to 50% bad lines the extra 0.034 s over about 100,000 bad lines means roughly 0.3 microseconds per raised and caught exception. Part of EAFP's base cost is the extra function call to parse_line, not the try itself.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
841 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 126 0 R
/T (Conclusion: in my measurements LBYL was faster at every bad-line rate \(1.5x at 0.1%, 3.6x at 50%\), partly because my EAFP version also pays for a function call per line. The key point is the trend: LBYL cost does not depend on how many lines are bad, while EAFP cost grows with every exception raised \(about 0.3 microseconds each\). So EAFP is acceptable when bad lines are rare and gives clearer code \(one place that describes the error\); when many lines are bad, explicit checks are clearly cheaper. For files, EAFP is also safer: checking os.path.exists\(\) first leaves a gap in which the file can disappear before open\(\). Space is O\(1\) per line in both.)
/E (Conclusion: in my measurements LBYL was faster at every bad-line rate \(1.5x at 0.1%, 3.6x at 50%\), partly because my EAFP version also pays for a function call per line. The key point is the trend: LBYL cost does not depend on how many lines are bad, while EAFP cost grows with every exception raised \(about 0.3 microseconds each\). So EAFP is acceptable when bad lines are rare and gives clearer code \(one place that describes the error\); when many lines are bad, explicit checks are clearly cheaper. For files, EAFP is also safer: checking os.path.exists\(\) first leaves a gap in which the file can disappear before open\(\). Space is O\(1\) per line in both.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
