1303 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 133 0 R
/T (Import forms \(demonstrated in the output below\): import attendance.stats loads the module and binds only the name attendance, so you must write attendance.stats.percentage\(...\); from attendance import stats binds the name stats to the sub-module, so you write stats.percentage\(...\); from attendance.stats import percentage binds the function itself, so you write percentage\(...\). All three give the same function object \(output: True\). The first keeps the origin visible, the last is shortest but can hide where a name came from or collide with a local name.)
/E (Import forms \(demonstrated in the output below\): import attendance.stats loads the module and binds only the name attendance, so you must write attendance.stats.percentage\(...\); from attendance import stats binds the name stats to the sub-module, so you write stats.percentage\(...\); from attendance.stats import percentage binds the function itself, so you write percentage\(...\). All three give the same function object \(output: True\). The first keeps the origin visible, the last is shortest but can hide where a name came from or collide with a local name.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [48]
>>
endobj
1304 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 133 0 R
/T (Honest reading of the change effort: the single script needed the fewest file edits \(1\) but it is also the one where unrelated code sits in the same file; splitting into modules costs more files touched but each edit is small and local. For a small program, \(a\) or \(b\) is enough; as the program grows, \(d\) pays off.)
/E (Honest reading of the change effort: the single script needed the fewest file edits \(1\) but it is also the one where unrelated code sits in the same file; splitting into modules costs more files touched but each edit is small and local. For a small program, \(a\) or \(b\) is enough; as the program grows, \(d\) pays off.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [49]
>>
