1388 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 134 0 R
/T (import cost: the first import attendance.stats finds, compiles \(or loads cached bytecode\), and executes the module once: measured 0.755 ms and 13.1 KiB of allocated memory for the three sub-modules. Afterwards the module object is stored in sys.modules, so repeating the import is a dictionary lookup: 0.133 microseconds each. So import cost is a one-time O\(size of the code\) and O\(1\) afterwards, and the memory is small and constant \(each module is held once, however many files import it\).)
/E (import cost: the first import attendance.stats finds, compiles \(or loads cached bytecode\), and executes the module once: measured 0.755 ms and 13.1 KiB of allocated memory for the three sub-modules. Afterwards the module object is stored in sys.modules, so repeating the import is a dictionary lookup: 0.133 microseconds each. So import cost is a one-time O\(size of the code\) and O\(1\) afterwards, and the memory is small and constant \(each module is held once, however many files import it\).)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [49]
>>
endobj
1389 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 134 0 R
/T (Finding and changing a function: in a single script of n lines you may scan O\(n\) lines \(53 here, and thousands in a real project\); in the package you go straight to attendance/stats.py \(16 lines\) because the function is named by its module. The time/space of the program itself is unchanged by the organisation; the benefit is human effort, testing and reuse, while the cost is a few more files and a sub-millisecond one-time import.)
/E (Finding and changing a function: in a single script of n lines you may scan O\(n\) lines \(53 here, and thousands in a real project\); in the package you go straight to attendance/stats.py \(16 lines\) because the function is named by its module. The time/space of the program itself is unchanged by the organisation; the benefit is human effort, testing and reuse, while the cost is a few more files and a sub-millisecond one-time import.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [50]
>>
