2641 0 obj
<<
/Title (Look Before You Leap \(LBYL\): test with if len\(parts\) == 3 and parts[2] in \('P','A'\) before using the line \(lbyl\(\)\), instead of raising and catching InvalidRecord \(eafp\(\), EAFP = easier to ask forgiveness\). Time per line is O\(1\) in both styles; the constant differs.)
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2628 0 R
/Next 2650 0 R
>>
endobj
2642 0 obj
<<
/Title (Conclusion: in my measurements LBYL was faster at every bad-line rate \(1.5x at 0.1%, 3.6x at 50%\), partly because my EAFP version also pays for a function call per line. The key point is the trend: LBYL cost does not depend on how many lines are bad, while EAFP cost grows with every exception raised \(about 0.3 microseconds each\). So EAFP is acceptable when bad lines are rare and gives clearer code \(one place that describes the error\); when many lines are bad, explicit checks are clearly cheaper. For files, EAFP is also safer: checking os.path.exists\(\) first leaves a gap in which the file can disappear before open\(\). Space is O\(1\) per line in both.)
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2628 0 R
/Prev 2650 0 R
>>
endobj
